<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  org.nextbsd.getty.ttyv0.plist

  A login on the FRAMEBUFFER console, alongside org.nextbsd.getty (which
  serves /dev/console).

  Why a second job at all — /dev/console is ONE device, not "both":
    org.nextbsd.getty.plist assumes /dev/console follows every active
    console, so a single job covers whatever the machine has. That is
    true of kernel MESSAGES: printf -> cnputc walks cn_devlist and
    writes to every attached console, which is what boot_multicons="YES"
    buys. It is NOT true of the console TTY. cninit() finishes with

        cnselect(best_cn);      /* highest cn_pri wins */

    and cnadd()/cnselect() call ttyconsdev_select(), which binds
    /dev/console to exactly ONE underlying tty. So on a dual-console
    boot you get messages on both and a login prompt on one.

  Why that bites arm64 specifically:
    The arm64 EFI loader reads the UART out of the ACPI SPCR table (or
    the DTB stdout-path) and hands it to the kernel as hw.uart.console
    — unprompted, on every arm64 machine, including ones whose owner
    never asked for a serial console:

        hw.uart.console="db:8,dt:pl011,mm:0x9000000,rs:2,rw:4,..."

    uart(4) then registers ttyu0 as a console, wins cnselect(), and
    /dev/console binds to it. amd64 sets no such tunable unless you ask
    (console=comconsole / boot_serial=YES), so there /dev/console is
    ttyv0 and the single getty lands on the screen. Same image, same
    plist, opposite result — and on an arm64 desktop or VM with no
    serial cable (qemu -machine virt with no -serial backend, UTM's
    default) the only login on the machine is written into a UART that
    nothing is listening to. The screen shows the kernel's boot
    messages, then goes quiet forever at the last line before userland
    (start_init: trying /sbin/launchd), which reads exactly like a
    hang. nextbsd#355 is a different bug in the same daemon: it fixed
    the post-login shell hanging on that UART, not the missing login.

  Why this is safe next to org.nextbsd.getty on amd64:
    Where /dev/console IS ttyv0, both jobs target the same tty. Measured
    on the arm64 image (a device-named getty raced against the running
    console getty): the loser exits 0 immediately and silently — no
    fight over the tty, no garbled prompts, and zero log lines in 30s —
    while the incumbent stays Ss+ and serves the login. Worst case the
    two jobs are duplicates and one no-ops; the user still sees exactly
    one prompt on the screen.

  Why the inline device test instead of a bare getty:
    A getty pointed at a device that does not exist (headless machine,
    no framebuffer, so vt(4) never attaches and /dev/ttyv0 is absent)
    fails, exits, and KeepAlive respawns it every ThrottleInterval
    forever — measured at 3 log lines per 30s, permanently. That is the
    missing-device respawn loop org.nextbsd.getty.plist's comment cites as
    the reason it serves /dev/console instead. The `test -c` guard makes
    the job a silent no-op on such machines (measured: 0 lines in 35s)
    without reintroducing a per-device wrapper SCRIPT — the guard is one
    line of the plist, and `exec` means the process launchd tracks IS
    getty, with the tty association it expects.

    KeepAlive's dict form would be the idiomatic Apple answer here
    (PathState => {"/dev/ttyv0" => true}), but this launchd does not
    implement it: with the path PRESENT and the getty killed, the job
    was not respawned. Treating KeepAlive as a dict silently disables
    keepalive, so it cannot be used as the guard.

  Why 3wire.9600 on a framebuffer tty:
    Carrier is irrelevant on vt(4) (it always asserts), so the nc
    capability that matters on a UART is merely harmless here. It is
    used anyway for consistency with org.nextbsd.getty, and because the
    curated /etc/gettytab has no Pc entry — the class stock FreeBSD
    /etc/ttys uses for ttyv* — so an unknown class would silently fall
    back to `default`. TERM is vt100, same as the console job.

  Scope: ttyv0 only, matching the one terminal a user sees at boot.
  FreeBSD's /etc/ttys runs gettys on ttyv0-ttyv7 so Alt-F2..F8 give
  more login screens; if that is wanted here it is one more job per
  terminal, and each is independently guarded.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>org.nextbsd.getty.ttyv0</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/sh</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>test -c /dev/ttyv0 || exit 0; exec /usr/libexec/getty 3wire.9600 ttyv0</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
